# Sunfront Solar Extractor — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Sunfront Solar Extractor, a sunlight harvester: a
fanned rank of angled solar-collector panels splayed off a canted spine on a low faceted
pedestal, drinking the sun into a glowing stored-sol core, as a 3D voxel model with a rig
a game runs at runtime. There is no target model to copy: build something that reads
unmistakably as this sunlight harvester and runs correctly from the description below.

The Solar Extractor is an **economy structure** — it harvests sunlight to bank the
legion's currency, produces **no units**, and has **no combat role**. It must read as a
passive collector, not a factory. This is a broad, low-slung harvester crowned by a wide
fan of panels — spend the volume on that fan of collectors, not on a tall tower.

This brief fixes what the Extractor is and how it must move. It deliberately does not
give you a parts list, joint placements, or pivots — working out how to split the
harvester into a fixed body and its moving pieces, where they attach, and how they sweep,
spread, and pulse is the challenge. Invent the rig.

## How the tool works

`voxel-anim` places discrete opaque cells. You paint solid material:

- Lay down cells with `set-voxel`/`fill-box` and the other cell operations (single
  voxels, filled and stroked boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and a mirror plane), each an
  opaque `#rrggbb` color; there is no transparency and no smoothing.
- Global `--part <name>` selects the part an op sculpts; each part is its own volume of
  cells, previewed on its own. Create a part with `define-part` before you sculpt into it.

Build one operation at a time. A call **records only** and renders nothing; run
`voxel-anim render` to (re)draw `parts/<part>.png` and the assembled `scene/*.png`, then
read them between calls. `voxel-anim --help` is the contract.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- **x** runs across the harvester, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. **y** runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to
  `{{voxel.maxY}}` (top). **z** runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- **Forward is +z:** the open face of the collector fan tilts up and toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}`
  (the front), so the panels face the sun. Up is +y.
- Build the harvester symmetric about the lengthwise vertical centerplane
  where the form allows, with the spine and fan centered on that axis.
- The extractor is deliberately broad and low — a squat faceted pedestal rooted to the
  ground, a canted spine leaning off it, and a wide fan of panels splayed across the top —
  so the whole form reads as a collector reaching for the sky, not a tower.
- Each part is composited in these shared coordinates, where it sits on the assembled
  harvester (the core banked in the pedestal, the spine rising from it, the fan splayed
  across its top).

## What the Solar Extractor is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the harvester must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A low, faceted **pedestal** — the fixed body of the machine — rooted to the ground, its
  footprint clearly **not a plain square** (canted, chamfered, or many-sided), built in
  the brass and bronze plating (bronze on its underside and in the shadowed seams,
  sandstone panels for lighter structure).
- A **canted spine** rising off the pedestal at a lean (not a vertical square mast) that
  carries the collector fan up and tilts it toward the sun, built in iron with brass
  bracing.
- A **fanned rank of angled collector panels** splayed across the top — several flat
  panels spread in a fan or sawtooth, each tilted to face the sky, their broad faces
  glowing solar-amber like photovoltaic cells in iron frames. This wide fan of collectors
  is the signature of the machine and must read as the largest feature.
- A **stored-sol core** — a glowing rounded reservoir of banked sunlight in the
  solar-amber and solar-hot accents, seated in or at the heart of the pedestal where the
  panels' harvest collects, sized to brighten and swell without touching its housing.
- A clear solar-amber energy accent and the palette below.

This is a harvester, not a factory: it must **not** carry a forge hammer, a blast door or
hatch, an assembly bay, a chimney, or any ejection port — it produces no units, and
nothing about it should look like it stamps out or releases anything.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette, proportions, how many panels
the fan holds and how they are shaped and splayed, how the spine is canted and braced,
how the core is shaped and housed, and how you break the harvester into rig parts and
place its joints. Nothing here prescribes a shape or a skeleton; the brief rewards a bold,
characterful design that is unmistakably the Solar Extractor and animates convincingly.
Leave the moving pieces something to seat against — a spine the fan pivots on, clear
hinges between the splayed panels, and a housing around the core — so they meet the
machine with no gap.

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Masonry — primary plating (brass) | `#c69a4b` |
| Masonry — dark plating, underside, shadow (bronze) | `#7a5527` |
| Secondary panels / lighter structure (sandstone) | `#d9c48c` |
| Frames, spine, joints, mechanisms (iron) | `#565c64` |
| Deep recesses, shadow (dark iron) | `#31353b` |
| Energy accent (solar amber) | `#ff9d2e` |
| Collector / core glow highlight (solar hot) | `#ffd76b` |

The **solar-amber** accent is the team-tint region: give the collector panel faces a
heavy, clear amber glow with a solar-hot highlight, and a brilliant solar-hot glow at the
stored-sol core — so the harvest reads as glowing, banked sunlight from multiple angles.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with three required animation declarations by name (you author
the motion). All three are self-playing idles — they loop continuously on their own, with
no caller — and the pedestal itself stays fixed throughout. Author each with `voxel-anim
define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, giving the motion an eased, weighted cadence rather
than a mechanical linear slide. None of these produces or releases a unit; they read only
as harvesting and banking sunlight.

- **`panel_track`** — the whole fan of collector panels slowly sweeps across the sky to
  follow the sun and eases back to where it started each loop, tilting as one to keep its
  faces to the light. The fan moves; the pedestal holds.
- **`collector_bloom`** — the splayed panels spread wider apart and draw back together
  each loop, the fan opening and closing in a slow breath like a flower turning to the
  light. The panels move; the pedestal and spine hold.
- **`sol_charge`** — the stored-sol core brightens and swells as harvested light gathers,
  then settles back each loop, breathing with weight. The core moves; the pedestal, spine,
  and fan hold.

You may add extra parts, joints, and self-playing animations of your own (a slow heat
shimmer, glinting facets, a secondary reflector); you must produce these three
animations, by these names, all self-playing, and must not contradict them (the pedestal
stays fixed — never carried along by the fan or the core; nothing about the motion reads
as manufacturing or emitting a unit).

## Working the tool

Define your parts with `define-part`, sculpt each with `--part <name>`, set pivots
with `set-pivot`, place joints with `define-joint`, and author the three animations'
keyframes — running `voxel-anim render` and reading `parts/<part>.png` and the
`scene/*.png` previews between calls to confirm the parts fit, the fan splays across the
spine with room to sweep and spread, the core sits housed with room to swell, and the
animations read with weight. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the
per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part scores as empty
(`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms>
--animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant to check the motion). The
recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are the authoritative output.
